 ],
 "literal": "Clear sea surrounded world, without common ownership, / white umbrella shading, even to those of sole rule, / middle of the night hour also, and day also, without sleeping, / swift beast watching, even to an unlearned person, / that which is eaten is one measure, those worn are two indeed, / other things all are exactly the same indeed. / Therefore, of wealth the benefit indeed is giving. / 'We will enjoy' if one thinks so, those that are lost are many indeed.",
 "translation": "Even for a great king who rules the entire world surrounded by the clear sea as his sole domain under his white umbrella, and for an unlearned hunter who does not sleep day or night as he watches for swift prey, the food eaten is but a single measure, and the garments worn are only two. All other basic needs are exactly the same. Therefore, the true purpose of wealth is to give to others; if one hoards it thinking 'I alone shall enjoy it,' much of it will only be lost.",
 "modern": "தெளிந்த கடலால் சூழப்பட்ட இவ்வுலகத்தை, பிறருக்குப் பொதுவானது என்று இல்லாமல், தன் ஒருவனுக்கே உரியதாக வெண்கொற்றக் குடையின் கீழ் ஆளும் மன்னனுக்கும்; நள்ளிரவிலும் பகலிலும் தூங்காமல், விரைந்து ஓடும் விலங்குகளை வேட்டையாடக் காத்திருக்கும் கல்லாத வேடனுக்கும்; உண்ணும் உணவு ஒரு நாழி அளவே ஆகும், உடுத்தும் ஆடைகள் இரண்டே ஆகும். மற்ற தேவைகள் யாவும் இருவருக்கும் சமமே. ஆதலால், செல்வத்தைச் சேர்ப்பதன் பயன் பிறருக்குக் கொடுப்பதே ஆகும். அவ்வாறு கொடுக்காமல், 'நாமே எல்லாவற்றையும் அனுபவிப்போம்' என்று எண்ணினால், அச்செல்வத்தில் அழியக் கூடியவை பலவாகும்.",
 "explanation": "The poet, Maturaik Kaṇakkāyaṉār Makaṉār Nakkīraṉār, offers a profound philosophical reflection on the equality of human needs and the true purpose of wealth. He compares a mighty emperor who rules the entire world under his white umbrella with an uneducated hunter who stays awake day and night to catch wild beasts. Despite their vast difference in status, both consume only a single measure of food and wear only two pieces of clothing. The contrast between the grand white umbrella of the king and the sleepless hunter highlights the universality of basic human limitations. The poem belongs to the potuviyal tiṇai and poruṇmoḻikkāñci theme, teaching that since physical needs are limited, the only true value of wealth lies in giving to others.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}